# API Integration Guidelines

## GitHub API Integration
- Use GitHub REST API v3 for fetching user and repository data
- Implement proper error handling with try-catch blocks
- Use loading states with skeleton components during API calls
- Cache API responses appropriately to avoid rate limiting
- Use proper TypeScript interfaces for API response types

## API Patterns
```typescript
interface ApiResponse {
  // Define proper interface structure
}

const fetchData = async (): Promise<ApiResponse[]> => {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(apiUrl);
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error("Failed to fetch data");
    }
    return await response.json();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("API Error:", error);
    throw error;
  }
};
```

## Component Structure for API Integration
- Use `"use client"` directive for components with useEffect/useState
- Implement loading, error, and success states
- Use Promise.all for parallel API calls when possible
- Provide fallback UI for error states
- Use proper loading indicators (skeleton components)

## GitHub API Endpoints Used
- `https://api.github.com/users/{username}` - User profile data
- `https://api.github.com/users/{username}/repos` - Repository list
- `https://api.github.com/users/{username}/events/public` - Recent activity

## Error Handling
- Display user-friendly error messages
- Implement retry mechanisms where appropriate
- Log errors for debugging purposes
- Provide fallback content when APIs are unavailable

## Performance Considerations
- Use React.memo for components that don't need frequent re-renders
- Implement proper cleanup in useEffect hooks
- Consider implementing caching strategies for frequently accessed data
- Use loading states to improve perceived performance